r  the sake of talking i once observed  decipher belnades that god had quite  gone out of fashion  god is now standing right in front of  you  and soon very very soon  he's going to have sex again the end  result is that we often spend large  chunks of time searching for a narrative  thread in a conversation that is simply  that  people talking it's not about moving the  plot forward so much as showing us a  grounded nuanced depiction of realistic  characters  now i know what some of you might be  saying chris for all this talk of  anti-narrative doesn't castlevania  employ some fairly standard narratives  throughout  and the answer is yes of course it does  the series finale for example  does ultimately give a lot of narrative  closure trevor  ends up surviving his literal fight with  death scifi is pregnant  you know what scared me the most what  then you might end up calling the kid  trevor  after all and alucard has finally found  a person who doesn't want to impale him  i knew you'd be strange but i didn't  think it would be something like this  that brought you back to life  it's always something like this pretty  much as close to a happily ever after  ending as you can get  that being said it would be nearly  impossible to create a tv series that  flips the bird to narrative altogether  if we did that it would never get past  the suits at the studios  but perhaps more importantly this  selective use of anti-narrative seems to  complement castlevania's video game  roots  there's something about a conversation  that drones on for way longer than a  normal scene that evokes the  endless dialogue chains you'd have with  an npc  and there's something about the lack of  closure to saint-germain's story that  makes you think  if you only could have played the tv  show like the game  you could have unpacked more of that  story that if you had the chance as a  player to interact with these characters  you'd dive even deeper into their  already considerably deep psycho